.TH NIBAR 1 "2026" "nibar 0.3.0" "NiWM manual"
.nh
.SH NAME
nibar \- vertical taskbar and applications menu for NiWM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nibar
.RB [ \-C
.IR nibarc ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR monitor ]
.RB [ \-r ]
.RB [ \-a ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR width ]
.RB [ \-f
.IR font ]
.RB [ \-b
.IR #bg ]
.RB [ \-c
.IR #fg ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nibar
is a bar that runs down the left edge of the screen. From top to bottom
it shows:
.TP
.B Apps
the applications menu (the chicken)
.TP
.B tags
a 3\(mu3 grid. The viewed tags are filled, tags holding windows have a
mark under the number, and urgent tags are amber.
.TP
.B layout
the current tag's layout
.TP
.B windows
the windows on the viewed tags. The focused one has a stripe on the
screen edge, and floating windows have a dot.
.TP
.B tray
icons from apps such as nm-applet or a volume control
.TP
.B status
battery and network, then a clock and the date
.PP
nibar follows NiWM through
.BR "niwmctl subscribe" ,
so it only redraws when something changes. It takes its accent colours
from NiWM's
.BR focus_color ,
.B normal_color
and
.BR urgent_color ,
so
.B niwmctl set focus_color
recolours the bar as well. It reserves its own space on screen, so
tiled windows never go under it.
.PP
If NiWM restarts, nibar reconnects by itself.
.SH CONFIGURATION
nibar reads the first of these files that exists:
.IP \(bu 2
the file given with
.B \-C
.IP \(bu 2
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/niwm/nibarc
.IP \(bu 2
.B ~/.config/niwm/nibarc
.IP \(bu 2
.B /etc/niwm/nibarc
.PP
Unlike niwmrc, a nibarc only needs the lines you change; anything left
out keeps its default. The stock
.B /etc/niwm/nibarc
lists every option with its default, commented out. To start your own:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
mkdir \-p ~/.config/niwm
cp /etc/niwm/nibarc ~/.config/niwm/nibarc
.fi
.RE
.PP
Each line is an option and a value. A
.B #
at the start of a line, or one with a space on both sides, starts a
comment. Mistakes are reported (see FILES) and the bad line is skipped.
.PP
nibarc is read again when NiWM reloads (mod+ctrl+r or
.BR "niwmctl reload" ),
or on
.BR "pkill \-HUP nibar" .
Command-line flags win over nibarc.
.SS Options
.TP
.BR position " left|right"
which screen edge (default left)
.TP
.BI width " px"
80 to 600 (default 150)
.TP
.BI monitor " N"
which monitor to sit on, 1 being the primary (default 1). The bar shows
that monitor's tags and windows; for a bar on every monitor, start one
nibar per monitor with
.BR \-m .
A bar whose monitor is unplugged hides until it comes back.
.TP
.BR layer " below|above|beside"
what happens when a window goes fullscreen.
.B below
(the default): it covers the bar.
.BR above :
the bar stays on top of it.
.BR beside :
it shrinks to fill the screen next to the bar.
.TP
.BI background " #rrggbb\fR, \fBforeground\fP #rrggbb"
the bar's colours (default #161214 and #e8dcd8)
.TP
.BR accent ", " border ", " urgent " auto|#rrggbb"
.B auto
(the default) follows NiWM's focus_color, normal_color and urgent_color.
The accent colours the viewed tag, the focus stripe, the menu border and
the chicken's comb.
.TP
.BI font " name"
an X core font; see
.B \-f
.TP
.BR logo " yes|no"
show the chicken on the menu button
.TP
.BI label " text"
the menu button's text (default Apps)
.TP
.BR windows " viewed|all"
list windows on the viewed tags only (the default), or on every tag
.TP
.BR battery ", " network " yes|no"
show those status rows
.TP
.BR clock " 24h|12h|off"
the pixel clock
.TP
.BI date " format\fR|\fBoff"
the date under the clock, as a
.BR strftime (3)
format (default %a %d %b)
.TP
.BR tray " yes|no"
the system tray (default yes)
.TP
.BI tray_size " px"
icon size, 12 to 48 (default 18)
.TP
.BI tray_hide " class..."
never show icons from these apps
.TP
.BI tray_show " class..."
if given, show only icons from these apps
.SS Example
.RS 4
.nf
layer beside
accent #3355ff
clock 12h
date %A %d
tray_hide Blueman\-applet
.fi
.RE
.SH SYSTEM TRAY
nibar is a freedesktop system tray, so apps that put an icon in a tray
(nm\-applet, volume controls, chat apps) show it in the bar, above the
battery. Clicks on an icon go to its app.
.PP
.B tray_hide
and
.B tray_show
choose icons by the app's class name, ignoring case. When an icon docks,
nibar prints its class, for example:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
nibar: tray icon 'Nm\-applet' (instance 'nm\-applet')
.fi
.RE
.PP
Either name works. A hidden icon stays docked, so its app keeps running
normally. If another tray (stalonetray, trayer) is already running,
nibar leaves the tray to it. When nibar exits, it hands the icons back
so their apps can dock them again.
.SH MOUSE
.TP
.B Apps
left click opens or closes the menu
.TP
.B a tag
left click views it, right click adds it to the view, middle click
moves the focused window there
.TP
.B layout
left click or wheel down: next layout. Right click or wheel up:
previous layout.
.TP
.B a window
left click jumps to it, middle click closes it
.SH THE MENU
The menu lists every program that has a
.B .desktop
file in
.BR ~/.local/share/applications ,
.B /usr/local/share/applications
or
.BR /usr/share/applications ,
grouped by category. It is read again each time it opens, so programs
you just installed are there straight away.
.PP
Open it by clicking
.BR Apps ,
or with
.B mod+p
in the stock niwmrc, which runs
.BR "niwmctl emit menu" .
.B pkill \-USR1 nibar
also works.
.PP
Start typing to filter by name. Then:
.TP
.B Up/Down, Tab, Page Up/Down, Home/End
move the selection
.TP
.B Enter
or a left click launches the selection
.TP
.B Backspace
deletes a character;
.B ctrl+u
clears the filter
.TP
.B Escape
or a click anywhere else closes the menu
.PP
Programs marked
.B Terminal=true
open in your terminal, which is found the same way as
.BR mod+Return .
The
.B NiWM
category at the end has
.B Reload config
and
.BR "Quit NiWM" .
.PP
To add your own entry, put a file like this in
.BR ~/.local/share/applications/mytool.desktop :
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
[Desktop Entry]
Type=Application
Name=My tool
Exec=mytool \-\-flag
Categories=Utility;
.fi
.RE
.PP
A file with the same name as a system one replaces it, and
.B NoDisplay=true
hides an entry.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-C " file"
read this nibarc instead of the usual ones
.TP
.BI \-m " N"
sit on monitor N, as with
.B monitor
in nibarc
.TP
.B \-r
sit on the right edge instead of the left
.TP
.B \-a
list windows from every tag, with each window's tag number
.TP
.BI \-w " px"
width in pixels (default 150)
.TP
.BI \-f " font"
an X core font. Use a monospaced one. The default is misc-fixed 6x13,
which handles accented and non-Latin text when
.B xfonts-base
is installed.
.TP
.BI \-b " #rrggbb"
background colour (default #161214)
.TP
.BI \-c " #rrggbb"
text colour (default #e8dcd8)
.SH EXAMPLES
In niwmrc:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
autostart nibar
autostart nibar \-r \-w 180
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
nibar's messages (nibarc mistakes, tray icon names) go to the same place
as NiWM's, since NiWM starts it. With
.B exec niwm\-x11 2> \(dq$HOME/.niwm.log\(dq
in ~/.xinitrc, that is
.BR ~/.niwm.log .
.SH SIGNALS
SIGHUP re-reads nibarc. SIGUSR1 opens or closes the menu. SIGTERM exits,
handing tray icons back first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR niwm (1)
